# Minab

Minab is a language with a pipeline query layer (`FROM`/`JOIN`/`LEFTJOIN`/`CROSSJOIN`/`WHERE`/`GROUPBY`/`HAVING`/`SELECT`/`ORDERBY`/`LIMIT` — note the SQL-style clause keywords with a multi-word name are single compound tokens, no space), a validation layer (a bare `.`-using expression is a record-level rule, a bare `$`-using expression is a field-level rule — there is no `VALIDATE`/`field` wrapper keyword), a full type system, user-defined functions, `if`/`if!`/`switch` control flow, three loop forms, and declarative `INSERT`/`UPDATE`/`DELETE` writes — all sharing one expression grammar. It's implemented with Langium (TypeScript).

Read before any non-trivial change:
- `docs/query-language-spec.md` — the language spec (12 sections), source of truth for syntax and semantics. §11 is the grammar reference; §12 is the list of genuinely open design questions — don't treat anything logged there as settled.
- `docs/showcase.md` — a runnable example for every construct in the spec, organized in the same order. Kept in sync with the spec on purpose: when the grammar changes, both files change in the same pass. When in doubt about whether a piece of syntax is valid, check here before guessing.
- `docs/roadmap.md` — the phased implementation plan, with a defined output per step.

Sigils and keywords, current as of the v2 spec (2026-09-13) — don't reach for the older `@alias` sigil or two-token `GROUP BY`/`LEFT JOIN`/`CROSS JOIN`, both superseded:
- `.` current record, `$` field value (field-level rules only), `^` parent record (one level up), `#alias` a named scope (opens any table inline, or disambiguates a join alias), `KEY` the group key.
- `GROUPBY`, `ORDERBY`, `LEFTJOIN`, `CROSSJOIN` are each one token — no space, no separate `GROUP`/`BY`/`LEFT`/`CROSS` keywords floating around to collide with identifiers elsewhere.
- `if` is an expression (every branch a full block, `else` optional and makes the type nullable); `if!` is a separate statement keyword for pure control flow — the two are different tokens, not the same keyword used two ways.
- `&name(...)` calls a user-defined function; a bare `name(...)` (no `&`) is a built-in (`COUNT`, `SUM`, `EXISTS`, ...).

Hard constraints, all the time:
- No implicit type coercion, anywhere in the language — cross-type comparisons need an explicit `CAST`.
- Every grammar change must be checked for LL(k) parsing safety before it's considered done (`npm run langium:generate` fails loudly on real conflicts — that's a blocker, not a warning to route around) — but generation succeeding is not the same as the grammar being correct. Actually run `npm test` too; the 2026-09-13 integration found three real defects (a clause-order regression, a missing `vivify` flag, an `isnot` misuse) that `langium generate` and `tsc` both passed cleanly on, and only the parse tests caught. See `docs/roadmap.md` Phase 1 for what they were.
- Keyword casing is a deliberate, load-bearing convention: SQL-style pipeline keywords are UPPERCASE (`FROM`, `WHERE`, `GROUPBY`, `SELECT`, `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, ...); loop/JSON/control-flow/function keywords are lowercase (`let`, `fn`, `if`, `if!`, `loop`, `switch`, `is`, `isnot`, `null`, ...). After any grammar addition, grep for collisions between the two sets.
- `docs/query-language-spec.md` and `docs/showcase.md` are controlled artifacts, and so is the grammar they document. Do not change any of them on your own judgment — including changes that look like obvious bugs. See the spec-governance rule for the required approval cycle, and for the narrow exception (an example that contradicts the spec's own unambiguous prose, like the three found on 2026-09-13, can be fixed directly — see that rule for what qualifies).
- Eighteen items are logged as genuinely open in spec §12 (types range from AST-shape questions to "no constants yet"). Don't silently resolve any of them either way — surface it and ask, following the spec-governance cycle.
